{"candidates": [{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate implicit invariants that are nowhere documented. Each locally correct change preserves the invariants its authors know about, but over time the set of true invariants grows beyond any individual's working knowledge, making changes increasingly likely to violate an unknown constraint.", "probability": 0.09}, {"conjecture": "The coupling in a software system follows a preferential attachment process: modules that are already widely depended on attract new dependencies because they appear to be stable infrastructure. This creates an irreversible rich-get-richer dynamic where core modules become change-aversion bottlenecks.", "probability": 0.08}, {"conjecture": "Every change to a system optimizes for local simplicity given the current context, but this locally greedy process produces a globally entangled structure. The system becomes harder to change not because of technical debt accumulation but because the search space of safe modifications grows combinatorially with interconnection density.", "probability": 0.07}, {"conjecture": "Tests encode a partial specification that becomes increasingly stale as the system's behavior evolves. Each change is verified against tests that captured past intent rather than current intent, so the test suite's pass/fail signal degrades in information value over time, making it harder to distinguish safe from unsafe changes.", "probability": 0.06}, {"conjecture": "Code review validates changes against the reviewer's mental model, but mental models diverge from system reality at a rate proportional to system size. As the system grows, reviews become increasingly based on incomplete models, so locally correct changes gradually introduce inconsistencies that accumulate into structural rigidity.", "probability": 0.05}, {"conjecture": "Long-running systems become harder to change because of an analog to the second law of thermodynamics: each change increases the entropy of the system's dependency graph, and there is no economically feasible reverse process that locally reduces entropy. The system drifts toward maximum-coupling configurations from which no single change can escape.", "probability": 0.04}, {"conjecture": "The difficulty increase is driven by the survival bias of changes: changes that increase coupling are more likely to be locally useful and thus accepted, while changes that reduce coupling have no immediate visible benefit and are deprioritized. Over time this selection pressure systematically increases the system's change cost.", "probability": 0.08}, {"conjecture": "As a system ages, the ratio of tacit knowledge to explicit knowledge about its behavior increases, because documentation decays faster than code changes. The system becomes harder to modify because an increasing fraction of the knowledge needed to make safe changes exists only in the heads of people who may leave.", "probability": 0.06}, {"conjecture": "Long-running systems accumulate multiple overlapping abstraction layers, each added to simplify a previous layer's complexity. The layers interact in ways no single layer designer anticipated, creating emergent constraints that are invisible at any single layer but collectively rigidify the system.", "probability": 0.05}, {"conjecture": "The problem is fundamentally one of growing semantic distance between the original design intent and the current code structure. Each locally correct change drifts the implementation slightly away from the conceptual model it was designed around, and eventually no coherent model explains the system, making every change require reverse-engineering rather than design reasoning.", "probability": 0.07}]}
